<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish reconquest of Santo Domingo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> was the campaign of 1808–1809 in which Dominican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-sanchez-ramirez">Juan Sánchez Ramírez</a>, with British naval help, drove the French from the eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1795. After victory at Palo Hincado in November 1808 they besieged the city of Santo Domingo, which surrendered in July 1809, and the colony returned to Spanish rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-reconquest-of-santo-domingo.jpg" alt="Spanish reconquest of Santo Domingo" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Risantana · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hispaniola_history_map_18stage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Treaty of Basel, signed on 22 July 1795, gave the Spanish part of the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but the handover was put off amid the turmoil of the slave uprising in the French west, and many Dominican families left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and Venezuela. Soldiers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> took possession in 1801, and the expedition of General Charles Leclerc followed in 1802. After Haitian independence in 1804 the east stayed French under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-louis-ferrand">Jean-Louis Ferrand</a>, who displaced François Kerverseau on 1 January 1804.</p><p>In 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> rising of 2 May and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> abdications led to juntas loyal to Ferdinand VII. Their proclamations reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in July 1808 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> within days, and several conspiracies against the French began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Agents from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> landed at Barahona on 23 September 1808 and stirred the south with Ciriaco Ramírez, but leadership passed to the east, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-sanchez-ramirez">Juan Sánchez Ramírez</a>, a landowner from Cotuí, entered El Seibo on 26 October with twenty-one men. At Palo Hincado on 7 November the night's rain had wet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, and the fight became a hand-to-hand struggle of French bayonets against Dominican machetes. French accounts also blamed the defection of Tomás Ramírez with two hundred cavalry. Ferrand died after the defeat.</p><p>The siege of the capital began on 15 November, while the British frigate under Captain Charles Dashwood took Samaná. Reinforcements came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>, ruler of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, used the confusion to occupy San Rafael and San Miguel. In June 1809 the British Major-general Hugh Lyle Carmichael landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a> with troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, and the 1,200-man garrison of Joseph-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> de Barquier surrendered to the British rather than to the Spaniards, its men shipped to Port Royal, Jamaica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Spanish rule returned in the period known as España Boba, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1815 restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> de jure. The colonial treasury stayed short, and in 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">José Núñez de Cáceres</a> declared independence; the Haitian army of Jean Pierre Boyer then began an occupation of 22 years, ended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-trinitaria">La Trinitaria</a> with the birth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> on 27 February 1844. Though not a war of independence, the reconquest counts among the early Dominican struggles against European rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish reconquest of Santo Domingo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
